>= 0: True\nMax probability: 0.947506\nMin probability: 0.0000000000\nNumber of very small probabilities: 2\nSmall test failed with analytical approach.\nForward KL error: 7.914611\nBackward KL error: 1.013070", "extras": {"command": "python analytical_solution.py", "metadata": {"exit_code": 0, "pid": -1, "username": "root", "hostname": "rack-ge107-ya-20250517193277", "working_dir": "/app", "py_interpreter_path": "/opt/openhands-venv/bin/python", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"}, "hidden": false}, "success": true}
{"id": 47, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T16:17:56.787129", "source": "agent", "message": "I am thinking...: I need to think more carefully about this problem. Let me approach it from a completely different angle using mathematical insight about the relationship between these KL divergences.\n\n\nLet me think about this problem more systematically. I need both KL(P||U) = 10 and KL(U||P) = 10.\n\nFor a vocab size V, uniform distribution U has U(i) = 1/V for all i.\n\nKL(P||U) = Σ P(i) * log(P(i) / (1/V)) = Σ P(i) * log(P(i)) + log(V) = -H(P) + log(V)\n\nKL(U||P) = Σ (1/V) * log((1/V) / P(i)) = -log(V) - (1/V) * Σ log(P(i))\n\nSo we need:\n1. -H(P) + log(V) = 10\n2. -log(V) - (1/V) * Σ log(P(i)) = 10\n\nFrom (1): H(P) = log(V) - 10\nFrom (2): (1/V) * Σ log(P(i)) = -log(V) - 10\n\nFor V = 150,000: log(V) ≈ 11.918\nSo we need H(P) ≈ 1.918\n\nThis is a very low entropy! The uniform distribution would have H(U) = log(V) ≈ 11.918, so we need a distribution that's much more concentrated.\n\nLet me try a different approach: use Lagrange multipliers or try to find a parametric form that naturally satisfies these constraints.\n\nActually, let me try a much simpler approach: a distribution with just one or a few elements having most of the probability mass.\n\n", "action": "think", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "toolu_22", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_01XUjDdQZ9oJWSV3V1GD32Rj", "created": 1766161076, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "I need to think more carefully about this problem. Let me approach it from a completely different angle using mathematical insight about the relationship between these KL divergences.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"thought\": \"\\n\\nLet me think about this problem more systematically. I need both KL(P||U) = 10 and KL(U||P) = 10.\\n\\nFor a vocab size V, uniform distribution U has U(i) = 1/V for all i.\\n\\nKL(P||U) = \\u03a3 P(i) * log(P(i) / (1/V)) = \\u03a3 P(i) * log(P(i)) + log(V) = -H(P) + log(V)\\n\\nKL(U||P) = \\u03a3 (1/V) * log((1/V) / P(i)) = -log(V) - (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i))\\n\\nSo we need:\\n1. -H(P) + log(V) = 10\\n2. -log(V) - (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i)) = 10\\n\\nFrom (1): H(P) = log(V) - 10\\nFrom (2): (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i)) = -log(V) - 10\\n\\nFor V = 150,000: log(V) \\u2248 11.918\\nSo we need H(P) \\u2248 1.918\\n\\nThis is a very low entropy! The uniform distribution would have H(U) = log(V) \\u2248 11.918, so we need a distribution that's much more concentrated.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: use Lagrange multipliers or try to find a parametric form that naturally satisfies these constraints.\\n\\nActually, let me try a much simpler approach: a distribution with just one or a few elements having most of the probability mass.\\n\\n\"}", "name": "think"}, "id": "toolu_22", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 453, "prompt_tokens": 26158, "total_tokens": 26611, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}, "extra_tokens_details": {"CacheCreationInputTokens": 26155}}, "service_tier": null}, "total_calls_in_response": 1}, "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0, "max_budget_per_task": null, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "default", "prompt_tokens": 287314, "completion_tokens": 12082, "cache_read_tokens": 40290, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "context_window": 0, "per_turn_token": 26611, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"thought": "I need to think more carefully about this problem. Let me approach it from a completely different angle using mathematical insight about the relationship between these KL divergences.\n\n\nLet me think about this problem more systematically. I need both KL(P||U) = 10 and KL(U||P) = 10.\n\nFor a vocab size V, uniform distribution U has U(i) = 1/V for all i.\n\nKL(P||U) = Σ P(i) * log(P(i) / (1/V)) = Σ P(i) * log(P(i)) + log(V) = -H(P) + log(V)\n\nKL(U||P) = Σ (1/V) * log((1/V) / P(i)) = -log(V) - (1/V) * Σ log(P(i))\n\nSo we need:\n1. -H(P) + log(V) = 10\n2. -log(V) - (1/V) * Σ log(P(i)) = 10\n\nFrom (1): H(P) = log(V) - 10\nFrom (2): (1/V) * Σ log(P(i)) = -log(V) - 10\n\nFor V = 150,000: log(V) ≈ 11.918\nSo we need H(P) ≈ 1.918\n\nThis is a very low entropy! The uniform distribution would have H(U) = log(V) ≈ 11.918, so we need a distribution that's much more concentrated.\n\nLet me try a different approach: use Lagrange multipliers or try to find a parametric form that naturally satisfies these constraints.\n\nActually, let me try a much simpler approach: a distribution with just one or a few elements having most of the probability mass.\n\n"}}
{"id": 48, "timestamp": "2025-12-19T16:17:56.790556", "source": "agent", "message": "Your thought has been logged.", "cause": 47, "observation": "think", "tool_call_metadata": {"function_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "toolu_22", "model_response": {"id": "msg_vrtx_01XUjDdQZ9oJWSV3V1GD32Rj", "created": 1766161076, "model": "claude-sonnet-4", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "I need to think more carefully about this problem. Let me approach it from a completely different angle using mathematical insight about the relationship between these KL divergences.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [{"index": 1, "function": {"arguments": "{\"thought\": \"\\n\\nLet me think about this problem more systematically. I need both KL(P||U) = 10 and KL(U||P) = 10.\\n\\nFor a vocab size V, uniform distribution U has U(i) = 1/V for all i.\\n\\nKL(P||U) = \\u03a3 P(i) * log(P(i) / (1/V)) = \\u03a3 P(i) * log(P(i)) + log(V) = -H(P) + log(V)\\n\\nKL(U||P) = \\u03a3 (1/V) * log((1/V) / P(i)) = -log(V) - (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i))\\n\\nSo we need:\\n1. -H(P) + log(V) = 10\\n2. -log(V) - (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i)) = 10\\n\\nFrom (1): H(P) = log(V) - 10\\nFrom (2): (1/V) * \\u03a3 log(P(i)) = -log(V) - 10\\n\\nFor V = 150,000: log(V) \\u2248 11.918\\nSo we need H(P) \\u2248 1.918\\n\\nThis is a very low entropy! The uniform distribution would have H(U) = log(V) \\u2248 11.918, so we need a distribution that's much more concentrated.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: use Lagrange multipliers or try to find a parametric form that naturally satisfies these constraints.\\n\\nActually, let me try a much simpler approach: a distribution with just one or a few elements having most of the probability mass.\\n\\n\"}", "name": "think"}, "id": "toolu_22", "type": "function"}], "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 453, "prompt_tokens": 26158, "total_tok